  "tour": {
    "title": "Revelation 1: An unveiling of Jesus to suffering churches",
    "intro": "This tour walks through Revelation 1 in the order the chapter itself unfolds: what kind of book this is, who first received it, what its opening blessing asks of hearers, and finally the overwhelming vision of Jesus among the lampstands. Listen for how the chapter moves from symbol and setting toward courage: John is not given a puzzle detached from life, but a vision meant to help real churches remain faithful.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:jewish-apocalyptic-imagination",
        "startSec": 333,
        "endSec": 633,
        "why": "Begin here for the chapter’s literary and theological orientation. The speakers trace the chain from “God the Father gave Jesus” to the messenger and John, and explain that Revelation is simultaneously “an apocalypse,” “a prophecy,” and a letter to seven churches. This gives you a useful lens before hearing the symbols themselves.",
        "episodeTitle": "The Jewish Apocalyptic Imagination",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/96e226f5-2108-4e87-b65d-1640239914c5/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=96e226f5-2108-4e87-b65d-1640239914c5",
        "durationSec": 2299,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:33ac7cf4-com-a16412d9-1281-3664-b0a3-f7637d157ed4",
        "startSec": 126,
        "endSec": 426,
        "why": "This clip works through Revelation 1:1–2 and clarifies what “the revelation of Jesus Christ” means in the speaker’s reading: chiefly a revelation from Jesus, not merely information about him. It also explains that the message “must soon take place” addresses the original churches, and that the visions are communicated through “sign language or symbol language.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Revelation 1:1-8",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/g2bje5sw3qnx58mc/revelation_11-88z0zq.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1208,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:33ac7cf4-com-a16412d9-1281-3664-b0a3-f7637d157ed4",
        "startSec": 426,
        "endSec": 726,
        "why": "Now hear the rest of the opening frame, including the blessing on those who “read,” “hear,” and “keep” the prophecy. The speaker emphasizes that Revelation is not “merely telling the future,” but a prophetic call to remain faithful to Jesus, then walks through the greeting to the seven churches and Jesus as “the faithful witness” and “the firstborn from the dead.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Revelation 1:1-8",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/g2bje5sw3qnx58mc/revelation_11-88z0zq.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1208,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:c8a44698-com-3d2fc9b8-7af2-39c7-9759-f6ed48939efd",
        "startSec": 432,
        "endSec": 732,
        "why": "Finish with the vision in Revelation 1:12–18. The speaker explains the seven lampstands, the “one like a son of man” in priestly clothing, and the imagery of white hair, fire, bronze, many waters, and the two-edged sword as biblical language portraying exalted glory. The clip lands where the vision becomes personal: John falls “like a dead man,” and Jesus answers, “Do not be afraid,” identifying himself as “the first and the last” and “the living one.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Revelation 1:9-20",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/scf2mkmpf24yyv7p/revelation_19-209es8b.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1185,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these clips let Revelation 1 set its own agenda: hear the book as an unveiling, receive it as a word addressed to churches under pressure, and let the strange imagery direct your attention to the living Jesus. The chapter ends not with speculation but with his reassurance and authority: he was dead, is alive forever, and holds “the keys of Death and of Hades.”",
    "totalSeconds": 1200
  },
